#### ChatGPT
Yes, you are correct. In a typical scenario using Amazon S3 with a PHP application, the file manipulation involves a combination of actions on both the server side and the client side.

Here&#039;s a step-by-step breakdown of how it typically works:

User uploads images: When a user uploads an image through your PHP application, the file is first sent to your server. Your PHP code then uses the AWS SDK to upload the file from your server to the S3 bucket. After the upload is successful, you receive a URL (often called a &quot;presigned URL&quot; for better security) from S3 representing the location of the uploaded file.

Storing links in the database: Once you have the URL from S3, you typically store this URL in your database. This allows you to associate the uploaded file (in this case, an image) with the relevant database record (e.g., a material). So, in your database, you would store the URL of the image along with other information about the material.

Displaying images to users: When a user requests to view a material (or any other content that includes images), your PHP code retrieves the URL of the image from the database and includes it in the HTML response sent back to the user&#039;s browser. The HTML might include &lt;img&gt; tags with the source attribute set to the URL of the image stored in S3. This causes the user&#039;s browser to fetch the image directly from S3.

Direct retrieval of images from S3: The images are served directly from Amazon S3 to the user&#039;s browser when requested. This means that the user&#039;s browser communicates directly with S3 to fetch the images, reducing the load on your web server.

By following this approach, you offload the storage and delivery of images to Amazon S3, which is designed for efficient storage and high-speed delivery of static content like images. This helps improve the performance and scalability of your PHP application.

