Yes, you can store files in different buckets depending on the publication privacy choice of the user. Storing files in different buckets based on access levels is one way to implement access control and maintain separation between different types of content.

Here&#039;s how you could approach it:

Private Bucket: For files that are marked as private, you can store them in a dedicated private bucket. Each user would have their own folder or prefix within this bucket to store their private files. You can control access to these files using IAM policies or by generating presigned URLs for sharing.

Group Bucket: For files that belong to a specific group, you can store them in a separate group bucket. Each group would have its own folder or prefix within this bucket. Access to group files can be controlled using IAM policies that grant access to members of the group.

Public and Official Buckets: For files that are meant to be public or official, you can store them in separate buckets dedicated to public or official content. These buckets would be configured to allow public access, either by setting appropriate permissions on the bucket or by making the files publicly accessible via presigned URLs.

By storing files in different buckets based on access levels, you can enforce stricter access controls and ensure that only authorized users have access to sensitive content. Additionally, separating content into different buckets can help with organization and management of data, especially as your application grows in scale.

